@database "tool_approval"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: tool_approval.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Tool Approval"
@{h1}Tool Approval

Tool Approval decides every tool call of an agent before it runs. Deny rules
block a call, allow rules let it through, and in @{tt}ask@{utt} mode every
other call is put to the person watching the run in the web chat: the call
and its arguments appear under the run, with the buttons
@{b}Allow once@{ub}, @{b}Allow for this session@{ub} and @{b}Deny@{ub} --
in @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} numbered, answered by the next line typed. A
call nobody answers does not run. The plugin is loaded in the default
configuration, but it is off for every agent until the agent switches it on.
It has no tools and no panel of its own.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Approving a call  " link approving}
  @{"  Who is asked, and when not  " link attended}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Rules and modes  " link rules}
  @{"  Sub-agents, spawns and scripts  " link subruns}
  @{"  The hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node approving "Approving a call"
@{h1}Approving a call

A question is a line in the run's status rows: @{tt}Approve <tool>?@{utt}
followed by the arguments. Below it the chat draws a box with the call's
arguments, a field for a reason and the buttons. A call a sub-agent makes is
asked the same way, in the sub-agent's box inside the run.

@{h2}What the box shows

@{bullet}Every argument by name, in the order the model sent them. Nested
fields are named by their path (@{tt}opts.cmd@{utt}, @{tt}files[2]@{utt}); a
name with any other character -- a dot, a bracket, a space, a line break --
is shown in quotes, so a key @{tt}"opts.cmd"@{utt} cannot pass for the field
@{tt}cmd@{utt} inside @{tt}opts@{utt}.
@{bullet}One-line values as JSON. Values with line breaks (a script, a
heredoc) follow as a block, each line marked with @{tt}│@{utt}, so a line of
code cannot pass for another argument.
@{bullet}Invisible characters escaped: control characters, bidirectional
overrides, zero-width characters, line and paragraph separators appear as
@{tt}\\u202e@{utt} and the like. What you read is what runs.
@{bullet}At most about 4000 characters. Short values stand whole; long ones
lose their middle, never their head or tail, and the box says
@{i}Long values are shortened in the middle -- check what the call writes
before you allow it.@{ui} Past 300 values the rest are counted
(@{i}… and 12 more arguments not shown@{ui}); the top-level arguments stay
in view before the fields of a big nested one.
@{bullet}A red warning above the buttons when the call would start work that
passes no approval -- a sub-agent that has approvals off, Claude Code, a
state machine: @{i}agent 'x' runs WITHOUT tool approvals: the rules of this
run do not apply to its calls.@{ui}

The text is shown as text, never as markup.

@{h2}In agent-cli chat

The call is printed below the run's status rows: @{tt}? Approve <tool>?@{utt},
the arguments as the box shows them, a warning where there is one, the
decisions numbered as the box offers them. The next line you type answers
it: the number of a decision, the number and a reason for Deny
(@{tt}3 too wide@{utt} where Deny is 3 -- a script's call offers only Allow
once and Deny, so Deny is 2 there), or the decision's name
(@{tt}Deny@{utt}). Words go with Deny only: @{tt}2 files left@{utt} allows
nothing (@{tt}Not taken: a reason goes with Deny only@{utt}). The chat
repeats what it took: @{tt}» Deny: too wide@{utt}. A line that answers
nothing is not sent: the chat says what would, puts the line back on the
input line, and the call keeps waiting. Several calls at once come one after
the other, oldest first: only the one your next line answers is shown in
full, with its arguments.

A line answers the call that was shown when you began typing it. If that
call ends before you press Enter, the line answers nothing -- not the next
call, whose arguments you have not read. A bare number (@{tt}1@{utt}) is
dropped; anything else goes to the agent as a message. A line you began before any call appeared is such a
message too, and the call keeps waiting.

@{h2}The buttons

@{table}
Button | What happens
Allow once | this call runs; the next call of the same tool is asked again
Allow for this session | this call runs, and every later call of the same tool in this session runs without a question -- whatever its arguments. Not offered for a script
Deny | the call does not run. The reason you typed goes to the agent with the refusal
@{body}

"Allow for this session" is kept per tool (@{tt}server/tool@{utt}), not per
argument: allowing @{tt}terminal_execute@{utt} once for the session allows
every later command. A deny rule still wins over it. For a spawn without
approvals it is kept per target: allowing agent @{tt}x@{utt} does not allow
agent @{tt}y@{utt}. It lasts until the server restarts.

After a click the box says @{i}Sending…@{ui}, then @{i}Answered: Allow
once@{ui}. The row's last line takes the box down in every tab that shows the
run: @{tt}<tool>: allowed once by <you>@{utt}, @{tt}allowed for this session
by …@{utt}, @{tt}denied by … -- <reason>@{utt}.

@{h2}When the answer is refused

@{bullet}@{i}Not taken: No such question is waiting -- it was answered, timed
out or its run ended.@{ui} -- the buttons stay off.
@{bullet}@{i}Not taken: Only the user whose run asks, or an admin, may
answer.@{ui} -- you are signed in as someone else.
@{bullet}Any other refusal leaves the buttons on for a second try.

@{h2}When nobody answers

@{table}
Case | What happens to the call
no answer within ask_timeout (300 s by default) | not run; the row ends @{tt}no answer within 300 s, not run@{utt}
the tab was closed and not reopened within gone_after_seconds (15 s) | the question stops; the call gets the @{tt}unattended@{utt} answer -- not run by default
the run is stopped while it asks | not run; the chat reports the call cancelled
the server restarts | the run ends; nothing about the question or earlier session grants is kept
@{body}

A page reloaded while a question waits gets the box back within
@{tt}reask_seconds@{utt} (20 s): the question is sent again that often.

@{h2}Who may answer

The user the run belongs to, or an admin. Only a person's sign-in counts --
the chat's session, or a bearer token -- never an API key: a program that
answers the questions put to a person would make them pointless.

@{b}With authentication off, anyone who reaches the API can answer@{ub} --
another web page, and the agent itself through an HTTP tool or a shell with
@{tt}curl@{utt}. Approvals that must hold against the model need
authentication on.
@endnode

@node attended "Who is asked, and when not"
@{h1}Who is asked, and when not

A question is put only where a person can answer it: a run the web chat
started for a signed-in user (or with authentication off), while a tab reads
it, or a turn of @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} that shows its status. Everything
else is @{i}unattended@{ui}, and the call gets the
@{tt}unattended@{utt} answer: @{tt}block@{utt} (the default) or
@{tt}allow@{utt}.

@{table}
Started by | Asked?
the web chat, signed in or authentication off, while a tab reads the run | yes
the web chat, an anonymous visitor; or the tab was closed for gone_after_seconds | no
agent-cli chat, its status shown in colour, typed into a terminal | yes, in the terminal
agent-cli chat with @{tt}--no-status@{utt}, without colour (@{tt}--color never@{utt} or @{tt}text@{utt}, @{tt}NO_COLOR@{utt}, @{tt}TERM=dumb@{utt}), or with input that is not a terminal | no
a sub-agent, an agent called as a tool, a state machine activity under such a run | yes, in the run above it
an async sub-agent after the run above it ended | no
the OpenAI-compatible API, agent-run, a one-shot agent-cli, a run woken in a process of its own (@{tt}--woken@{utt}), a JSON @{tt}/run@{utt} | no
a call inside a tool_script script | never -- see @{" Sub-agents, spawns and scripts " link subruns}
@{body}

The web chat marks the runs it starts (@{tt}"attended": true@{utt} on
@{tt}POST /events@{utt}, form field @{tt}attended@{utt} on @{tt}/run@{utt}
with files), and so does @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} for a turn whose status
it shows in colour and whose keys it reads. Another client that shows its user the stream
must send the mark itself and draw the question from
@{tt}meta.tool_approval.form@{utt}; see @{" Setting it up " link setup}.

"Nobody reads" counts from the moment the last tab left the run, so a reload
-- which reads again within moments -- keeps the question. A spawn without
approvals is always blocked where nobody can be asked, whatever
@{tt}unattended@{utt} says.
@endnode

@node rules "Rules and modes"
@{h1}Rules and modes

@{h2}Modes

@{table}
Mode | Deny rules | Allow rules | Everything else
ask | block | run without a question | put to the person watching; unattended: the @{tt}unattended@{utt} answer
auto | block | -- | runs
off | the instance's deny rules still block | -- | runs
@{body}

@{tt}off@{utt} on an agent switches off its own rules and its questions; the
deny rules of the instance (the plugins.yaml entry) and those of a run above
it still apply. @{tt}off@{utt} on the instance is the default for agents
that set no mode, and for them nothing is checked. YAML reads a bare
@{tt}off@{utt} as false; both mean off.

@{h2}The order of the checks

@{bullet}A deny rule of the run, or of a run above it, names the call:
blocked. Deny wins over allow rules and over "allow for this session".
@{bullet}Mode off: the call runs.
@{bullet}The call starts work without approvals (a spawn): blocked in auto,
asked with a warning in ask. Allow rules do not let it through.
@{bullet}A call inside a script: runs.
@{bullet}Mode auto: runs.
@{bullet}Mode ask: an allow rule names it, or a person allowed the tool for
this session: runs. Otherwise the person is asked.

@{h2}A rule

A rule names tools the way an agent's @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} does, by the
same function:

@{table}
Pattern | Matches
server/tool | that one tool; the tool name carries the instance prefix: @{tt}terminal/terminal_execute@{utt}
server/* | every tool of that instance
server | the same, without the slash
anything with * | fnmatch on @{tt}server/tool@{utt} and on the bare tool name: @{tt}*_execute@{utt} matches the execute tool of every instance
@{body}

A rule names an @{b}instance@{ub}, not a plugin type:
@{tt}terminal/*@{utt} does not reach a second shell instance such as
@{tt}coder_shell@{utt}. Deny each instance, or use a wildcard. Without a
@{tt}*@{utt} the pattern is compared as it is, so @{tt}?@{utt} and
@{tt}[ ]@{utt} only work next to a @{tt}*@{utt}.

A rule may add patterns on the call's arguments:

@{code yaml}
deny:
  - "ssh_control"
  - tool: "terminal/*"
    arguments:
      command: "\\\\bsudo\\\\b"
allow:
  - "file_ops/*_read_*"
  - tool: "terminal/terminal_execute"
    arguments:
      command: "^git (status|log|diff)( [\\\\w./][-\\\\w./]*)*\\\\Z"
@{body}

Every argument pattern must match (@{tt}re.search@{utt}) for the rule to
match. A value that is not a string is matched as its JSON text. A call
without the argument does not match -- for a deny rule that means it is
not blocked by that rule.

@{tt}re.search@{utt} finds the pattern anywhere: an allow pattern
@{tt}^git status@{utt} also allows @{tt}git status; rm -rf ~@{utt}. Anchor
both ends (@{tt}^...\\Z@{utt}; @{tt}$@{utt} also matches before a final line
break) and leave out the shell's @{tt};@{utt}, @{tt}&@{utt}, @{tt}|@{utt},
@{tt}$@{utt} and backquote. Know the allowed command's options too:
@{tt}git diff --output FILE@{utt} writes a file, so the example allows no
argument that starts with @{tt}-@{utt}.

@{b}An argument rule on a shell is no boundary.@{ub} A regex catches the
spellings it was written for, not @{tt}rm -R@{utt}, @{tt}find -delete@{utt},
a script file, an alias or an interpreter that does the same. Use it to stop
the obvious; to keep an agent out of the shell, deny the tool.

@{h2}A rule that cannot be read

A bad regex, an unknown key, @{tt}arguments@{utt} that are not a mapping, a
mode or @{tt}unattended@{utt} value that does not exist, a number of seconds
that is not above 0: every call the hook decides for that agent is blocked,
and the error is logged. An error in the instance's settings is logged at
start and blocks every agent that switched the hook on. A typo in a deny
rule must not open what it was written to close.
@endnode

@node subruns "Sub-agents, spawns and scripts"
@{h1}Sub-agents, spawns and scripts

A run cannot leave its rules behind by starting other work.

@{h2}Sub-runs inherit

A sub-agent, an agent called as a tool, a state machine activity: each runs
under a request id that extends the id of the run that started it. The
spawning call passes the parent's hook first, which records the parent's
rules; a sub-run whose agent has the hook on is held to those of every run
above it, never looser:

@{bullet}mode: the stricter one (off < auto < ask). A parent in ask makes
the sub-run ask, in the parent's chat.
@{bullet}deny: every rule of every level.
@{bullet}allow: a call runs unasked only when an allow rule of @{i}every@{ui}
asking level names it.
@{bullet}unattended: block when any asking level says block.
@{bullet}"allow for this session": counts only where it was given for every
asking level; it is kept in the session of each and only for the run's
owner.

The recorded rules live in memory while the run or a run under it streams,
and a minute after its last call; at most 10000 runs.

@{h2}Spawns without approvals

Work whose calls this hook would not see is decided on its own, before allow
rules and session grants:

@{table}
Call | Starts | Without approvals when
sub_agent_manager manage_sub_agent, create | the agent @{tt}agent_type@{utt} | that agent does not run this hook, or is a state machine agent
sub_agent_manager manage_sub_agent, continue | the agent of the sub-session @{tt}instance_id@{utt} | the same; a sub-session that cannot be read counts as without
an agent called as a tool | that agent | the same; a schema agent's @{tt}list_available_tools@{utt} starts nothing
coding_cli run_task | Claude Code | always
stategraph run_machine, send_event, control_run continue/step/run_to/resume/fork/terminate | the machine | always: its @{tt}tool:@{utt} activities pass no hook
@{body}

Servers are recognised by their plugin type, whatever the instance is
called. "Does not run this hook" means this instance's
@{tt}check_tool_call@{utt}: a sub-agent guarded only by a second
tool_approval instance counts as without. In auto such a spawn is blocked;
in ask it is asked with the warning; unattended or inside a script it is
blocked.

Not covered: @{tt}agent_continuation@{utt} continues the same run.
@{tt}terminal@{utt}, @{tt}ssh_control@{utt} and the like can start anything,
an agent CLI included -- deny them to close that. A sub-agent started from a
slash command or a panel button has no request id of a caller and inherits
nothing.

@{h2}Scripts

A tool_script script is one call of the model: it passes this hook like any
other, asked with its code in the box, and only ever allowed once -- allowed
for the session, every later script and every call inside one would run
unread. The calls inside a script never ask: deny rules (the run's and the
inherited ones) and spawns without approvals block them, everything else
runs.
@endnode

@node hook "The hook"
@{h1}The hook

One @{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hook, @{tt}check_tool_call@{utt}. It runs before
every tool call of an agent that switched it on -- the model's calls, and the
calls a script makes -- after every hook that changes arguments (category
@{tt}tool_arguments@{utt}), so what it checks is what runs. Calls the
framework already rejected (unknown tool, malformed arguments) never reach
it.

It never changes a call. It lets it pass, or blocks it: the model reads the
refusal in place of a result (see @{" What the model sees " link model}).
It fails closed: a hook that crashes or runs past its own timeout (3600 s)
blocks the call.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML; the key is the full hook name,
@{tt}<instance>.check_tool_call@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      tool_approval.check_tool_call:
        enabled: true
        mode: ask
        allow:
          - "file_ops/*_read_*"
        deny:
          - "ssh_control"
        ask_timeout: 300
        unattended: block
@{body}

@{tt}hooks.enabled: false@{utt} on the agent switches every hook off, this
one too.

@{h2}Settings

Each key of the agent's override replaces the instance's @{tt}config:@{utt}
in plugins.yaml, which replaces the default -- except @{tt}allow@{utt} and
@{tt}deny@{utt}: the agent's rules are @{b}added@{ub} to the instance's, so
an agent cannot drop a deny rule the operator set for everyone.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
mode | ask | ask, auto or off; see @{" Rules and modes " link rules}
allow | [] | rules for calls that run without a question in ask
deny | [] | rules for calls that never run
ask_timeout | 300 | seconds a question waits; unanswered, the call does not run. Kept 5 s short of the hook's timeout (3600 s), with a warning in the log
unattended | block | block or allow: a call that would be asked while nobody can answer
gone_after_seconds | 15 | a run nobody has read this long is not asked any more; a waiting question stops
reask_seconds | 20 | a waiting question is sent again this often, for a reloaded page
@{body}

@{tt}enabled@{utt}, @{tt}timeout@{utt} and @{tt}order@{utt} in the override
belong to the hook system and do not reach the hook. The seconds must be
numbers above 0.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

No tool, no tool description, no injected message. A call that is allowed
runs as it was sent, and the model sees nothing of the check. The person's
waiting shows in the chat, not to the model.

A blocked call's result is the framework's

@{code json}
{"status": "error", "error": "<text>", "type": "ToolCallBlocked"}
@{body}

with one of these texts (@{tt}<tool>@{utt} is the name the model called):

@{table}
Case | Text
deny rule | The call to '<tool>' is not allowed for this agent (rule on <tool pattern>); it did not run. Do not send it again, and do not try to get the same effect with another call. Tell the user what you would need.
the person denied | The user denied the call to '<tool>'; it did not run. Their reason: <reason> Do not send it again, and do not try to get the same effect with another call. Take their answer into account, or ask the user how to proceed.
no answer | The call to '<tool>' needs the user's approval, and nobody answered within <n> seconds; it did not run. Do not send it again right away: go on without it, or finish and tell the user which call is waiting for their approval.
nobody watching | The call to '<tool>' needs a person's approval, and nobody can give it in this run: nobody is watching it. It did not run. Go on without it, or finish and tell the user which call needs their approval.
rules unreadable | The call to '<tool>' did not run: the approval rules of this agent cannot be read (<error>). Tell the user; do not retry it.
spawn, auto | The call to '<tool>' would start <target>, which runs without tool approvals: the rules this run is held to would not apply to its calls. It did not run. Use an agent that has approvals switched on, or tell the user that <target> needs them.
spawn, nobody to ask | The call to '<tool>' would start <target> without tool approvals; only a person may allow that, and nobody is watching this run. It did not run. Go on without it, or finish and tell the user which call needs their approval.
spawn inside a script, ask | The call to '<tool>' inside the script would start <target> without tool approvals, and only a person may allow that -- a script cannot ask. It did not run. Make this call on its own, outside the script, so the user can be asked.
@{body}

Without a reason the sentence @{i}Their reason: …@{ui} is left out. In
auto a spawn inside a script reads @{i}The call to '<tool>' inside the
script would start …@{ui}. @{tt}<target>@{utt} reads @{i}agent 'name'@{ui},
@{i}Claude Code (coding_cli)@{ui}, @{i}the state machine 'id'@{ui} or
@{i}the sub-agent 'instance'@{ui}. A call cancelled while it was asked is
reported cancelled by the framework, not blocked.

The deny text names the rule's tool pattern, never its argument patterns: a
regex in the result would be a recipe for the call that slips past it.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

A blocked call costs its text -- about 40 to 80 tokens, plus the person's
reason -- in place of the result the tool would have returned. The history
stays append-only: the model's call and the refusal stand in their place,
nothing earlier is rewritten, and the prompt cache holds.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The default configuration loads the plugin, so its answer route exists, and
switches the hook on for nobody (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    tool_approval:
      type: tool_approval
      enabled: true
      config:
        mode: ask
        ask_timeout: 300
        unattended: block
        # ask_user only puts a question to the same person: approving it first
        # would ask twice
        allow: ["ask_user/*"]
        deny: []
@{body}

The rules here hold for every agent that switches the hook on; each agent's
own are added. The instance's settings are read when the plugin starts: a
change takes effect after a restart. No tool, so no allowlist line; no extra
packages. Each agent opts in as shown in @{" The hook " link hook}.

@{h2}The routes

@{code text}
POST /plugins/tool_approval/answer    {"question_id", "decision", "reason"}
GET  /plugins/tool_approval/pending   [?session_id=…&request_id=…]
@{body}

@{tt}decision@{utt} is @{tt}allow_once@{utt}, @{tt}allow_session@{utt} or
@{tt}deny@{utt}; @{tt}reason@{utt} reaches the model with deny only, cut to
1000 characters. An unknown decision, or @{tt}allow_session@{utt} for a
script, answers 422; a question that no longer waits, 404; someone else's,
403. @{tt}/pending@{utt} lists the open questions the caller may answer;
@{tt}request_id@{utt} also takes those of the runs under it,
@{tt}session_id@{utt} those of the agents that session called as tools.

The question travels as a status line of the run, with
@{tt}meta.tool_approval@{utt}: @{tt}id, tool, server, arguments,
arguments_cut, warning, decisions, request_id, session_id, agent, asked_at,
expires_at, answer_url@{utt}, and @{tt}form@{utt} -- the question as every client draws it:
@{tt}prompt, detail, warning, choices@{utt} (each @{tt}value, label@{utt}),
@{tt}multi_select, text@{utt} (@{tt}label, alone@{utt}: whether words answer
without a choice, or null). A client other than the web chat draws the
question from @{tt}form@{utt}, posts the @{tt}value@{utt} of what was picked,
and sends @{tt}attended@{utt} when it starts a run; agent-cli chat does
exactly that, in its own process. For tool_approval the
choices are the decisions offered, the detail is the arguments, and the
words -- a reason -- go along with Deny only.

@{h2}Where data lives

Nowhere on disk. Open questions, the rules recorded for running sub-runs and
what was allowed for a session live in the server process; a restart ends
every run that waits and forgets every session grant. Session grants are kept
for the last 1000 sessions.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}"Allow for this session" is per tool, not per argument.
@{bullet}auto has no reviewer: unmatched calls run.
@{bullet}A long value is shortened in the middle in the box. When the part
that matters could be hidden there, deny and ask the agent for a shorter
call.
@endnode
